\section{Measuring Temporal Self-Consistency}
\label{sec:te}

\subsection{Metric}
\label{sec:te-metric}

An agent is \emph{temporally self-consistent} if the action it eventually takes at
turn $t$ is one it could already have predicted several turns earlier. We make this
operational as follows. Let $\tau = (o_1, a_1, \dots, o_T, a_T)$ be a trajectory, and
let $a_t$ denote the action emitted at turn $t$, tokenised as
$a_t = (a_t^1, \dots, a_t^{n_t})$.

For each \emph{target} turn $t$ we truncate the dialogue at each of the $k$ preceding
turns and ask the model to \emph{forecast} the action at turn $t$. Writing
$\pi_\theta(\cdot \mid \tau_{<s})$ for the model's distribution given the prefix up to
turn $s$, the \emph{forecast ensemble} is the uniform mixture over the $k$ members
\begin{equation}
  q^{F}(\cdot) \;=\; \frac{1}{k}\sum_{j=1}^{k} \pi_\theta\!\left(\cdot \;\middle|\; \tau_{<t-j}\right),
  \label{eq:qf}
\end{equation}
while $\pi^{0}(\cdot) = \pi_\theta(\cdot \mid \tau_{<t})$ is the model's
\emph{on-the-spot} distribution, conditioned on the full prefix that actually preceded
$a_t$. The per-token temporal gain at turn $t$ is
\begin{equation}
  g_t \;=\; \frac{1}{n_t}\Big[\log q^{F}(a_t) \;-\; \log \pi^{0}(a_t)\Big],
  \label{eq:gain}
\end{equation}
and the trajectory-level score is the mean of $g_t$ over the target turns of $\tau$.
Both terms are evaluated token-by-token over the full vocabulary. A value of $g_t = 0$
means the forecast made $k$ turns in advance assigns the realised action exactly the
probability the model assigns it once it is actually there; $g_t < 0$ quantifies how
much foresight is missing. Throughout we use $k = 3$ and mixture weight $\eta = 0.5$.

\paragraph{The metric is a difference, and must be reported as one.}
Equation~\eqref{eq:gain} is a difference of two log-probabilities, and it increases
under two mechanisms that carry opposite scientific meaning:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
  \item[(A)] $\log q^{F}(a_t)$ \emph{rises} --- the model genuinely anticipates the
        action earlier. This is the effect the metric is intended to capture.
  \item[(B)] $\log \pi^{0}(a_t)$ \emph{falls} --- the model has moved away from $a_t$
        and no longer considers it likely even in context. The gain rises without any
        improvement in foresight.
\end{enumerate}
Mechanism (B) is not hypothetical: it is the dominant term for one of the systems we
evaluate (\S\ref{sec:te-findings}). We therefore report $\Delta \log q^{F}$ and
$-\Delta \log \pi^{0}$ alongside every $\Delta g$, and treat a gain that is not
accompanied by a rise in $\log q^{F}$ as an artefact rather than a result.

\subsection{Evaluation protocol}
\label{sec:te-protocol}

Because $g_t$ is evaluated \emph{on} a trajectory, the choice of trajectories is part
of the measurement. We contrast two protocols.

\paragraph{Self-generated trajectories (\textsc{own}) are not a valid protocol.}
The natural choice --- score each checkpoint on the trajectories it produced --- matches
the quantity logged during training, but is confounded twice over. First, the target
set itself changes with the model: stronger checkpoints emit shorter, cleaner rollouts,
so the turns being scored are not the same turns. Second, and more damaging, a model
assigns near-certainty to its own outputs. We measure a mean per-token
$\log \pi^{0}$ of $-0.03$ to $-0.02$ (probability $0.97$--$0.98$) on self-generated
trajectories, against $-1.30$ on held-out ones. The denominator of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:gain} collapses, and $g_t \approx \log q^{F}(a_t)$ degenerates into
``how well does the model predict text it wrote itself''. As a direct demonstration,
holding the \emph{model weights fixed} and changing only the trajectory set moves the
untrained base model from $g = -5.30$ to $g = -1.78$, a shift of $+3.53$ --- larger
than the training effect of most checkpoints we evaluate. We report \textsc{own}
only as this negative control.

\paragraph{Fixed held-out trajectories.}
All models are instead scored on one shared, frozen set of trajectories, so that every
difference is attributable to the weights alone. Constructing this set requires care,
and we impose three constraints.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=2pt,topsep=2pt]
  \item \textbf{Successful trajectories only.} We admit only episodes that terminated
        with full task reward. An earlier version of this set, drawn from the base
        model, contained no successful episodes at all; its trajectories were dominated
        by action loops and round-limit truncations, precisely the behaviour RL removes
        first, which inflates mechanism (B).
  \item \textbf{Stratification by task type.} Trajectories are sampled across task
        families, covering $20$ of the $26$ types in the benchmark test split. Naive
        lexicographic file ordering --- the failure mode of our first attempt --- yields
        a contiguous block of task identifiers spanning only $4$ types.
  \item \textbf{Balanced provenance.} Successful trajectories necessarily originate
        from trained checkpoints. Drawing them all from one system would score that
        system on its own outputs, reintroducing the \textsc{own} confound. We
        therefore rotate provenance evenly across the three systems compared
        ($21/20/19$ of $60$).
\end{enumerate}

The resulting set contains $60$ trajectories and $1{,}428$ target turns, identical for
all $16$ models. We verify that balanced provenance suffices: recomputing every
$\Delta g$ after \emph{excluding} the trajectories contributed by the system under
evaluation leaves all conclusions unchanged, and in fact slightly strengthens the
positive effects (\S\ref{sec:te-findings}).

\paragraph{Uncertainty.} Target turns within a trajectory are strongly correlated, so
we take the trajectory as the resampling unit. All intervals are $95\%$ bootstrap
confidence intervals over $4{,}000$ resamples of the $60$ trajectories, computed on
paired per-trajectory differences against the base model.

\subsection{Results}
\label{sec:te-findings}

We evaluate an untrained base model and three systems at five checkpoints each:
plain GRPO, GRPO with an auxiliary \emph{plan-forecast} objective ($k=3$) that trains
the model to predict its own next $k$ actions, and that same objective augmented with
an explicit temporal-ensembling KL penalty (TE-KL, $\lambda = 10^{-3}$).
Table~\ref{tab:te} reports the change in temporal gain relative to the base model,
decomposed into the two mechanisms of \S\ref{sec:te-metric}.

