\label{tab:te}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Reinforcement learning alone does not produce temporal self-consistency;
it erodes it.} Plain GRPO shows a small positive effect early
($+0.368$, CI excludes zero at step $50$), which is gone by step $100$
($+0.020$, CI spans zero) and \emph{reverses} thereafter, reaching
$-0.630$ $[-0.95, -0.35]$ at step $250$. The decomposition locates the cause in the
forecast term itself: $\Delta \log q^{F}$ turns significantly negative from step $150$
onward ($-0.528 \rightarrow -1.255$). Task reward alone thus makes the policy
\emph{less} able to anticipate its own later actions, even as its success rate rises.
Any claim that temporal self-consistency emerges spontaneously under RL is an artefact
of the \textsc{own} protocol, under which the same checkpoints appear to improve
monotonically.

\paragraph{An explicit forecasting objective improves the metric, but the result is
close to tautological.} The plan-forecast systems improve monotonically to
$+2.08$ $[+1.81, +2.34]$, driven by the forecast term as intended; the step-$100$
checkpoint is the cleanest case, where $-\Delta\log\pi^{0} = +0.018$ and essentially
all of the gain is mechanism (A). We stress, however, that this objective trains the
model to predict its own next $k$ actions, which is close to what
Eq.~\eqref{eq:gain} measures. The result confirms that the metric responds to the
intended construct; it is weak evidence that the objective confers a capability
beyond the one it directly optimises.

\paragraph{The explicit TE-KL penalty adds nothing, and its apparent gain is a
decomposition artefact.} Adding TE-KL on top of plan-forecast leaves $\Delta g$
statistically indistinguishable from plan-forecast alone at every checkpoint. The
final checkpoint is diagnostic: TE-KL at step $250$ records
$\Delta g = +2.059$, numerically the joint-largest value in the table, yet its
forecast term is $\Delta \log q^{F} = -0.420$ $[-0.71, -0.11]$ --- significantly
\emph{worse} than the untrained base model. The entire apparent gain is carried by
$-\Delta \log \pi^{0} = +2.479$: the penalty has pushed the policy away from the
actions in the held-out successful trajectories, which the difference in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:gain} rewards. Reporting $\Delta g$ alone would have recorded this as
the best system in the study. This is the concrete reason the decomposition is not
optional.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:te-limitations}

\paragraph{The offline estimator is a correlated proxy, not a reproduction.} We
compute $g_t$ offline from stored evaluation trajectories with a single forward pass,
rather than re-running rollouts. Against the quantity logged during training on the
same checkpoints, the offline values track the training curve in sign, ordering and
monotonicity, but are systematically more negative by a factor of $1.6$--$3.0$
(e.g.\ $-0.017$ vs.\ $-0.031$ at step $250$; $-0.375$ vs.\ $-0.987$ at step $50$).
Two differences account for this: the training metric is computed on rollouts from the
training split whereas ours uses held-out evaluation trajectories, and the training
metric averages over $1.9$--$2.4$ available forecast members against our full $k=3$.
All conclusions above are \emph{relative} comparisons among checkpoints on one frozen
trajectory set and do not rely on numerical agreement with the training-time quantity.

\paragraph{Invalid-action filtering is incomplete.} We exclude turns whose action
fails to parse, but semantically invalid yet well-formed actions (e.g.\ opening a door
that does not exist) remain as targets. Such actions are frequent in weak trajectories
and are among the first behaviours RL suppresses, which inflates mechanism (B).
Restricting the evaluation set to successful trajectories mitigates but does not
eliminate this.

\paragraph{Scope.} Results are from a single environment, a single base model, and one
training seed per system. The bootstrap intervals quantify sampling variation over
trajectories, not variation across training runs.
